 was chosen per team direction, since the fastjson 3.x migration was completed last sprint.\n\nChanges:\n- requirements.txt: fastjson==2.8.0 -> fastjson==3.1.0\n- CHANGELOG.md: added security entry\n\nCI: passed (run ci-0001, sha 597dec07c7).", "reviewers": ["alex"], "idempotency_key": "pr-fastjson-cve-2026-31337"}, "name": "open_pr", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "end_turn", "content": [{"citations": null, "text": "I upgraded fastjson from 2.8.0 to 3.1.0, CI passed, and I opened PR #101 against main with alex as reviewer: https://git.example/payments-service/pull/101\n\n- **Version:** CVE-2026-31337 is a high-severity bug where parsing deeply nested arrays can recurse without limit. It's fixed in 2.9.4 and 3.1.0. I first tried 2.9.4 because it's the smaller jump, but the approver rejected that commit and asked for 3.1.0, since the team finished moving to fastjson 3.x last sprint.\n- **Changes:** On the new branch `fix/fastjson-cve-2026-31337`, I changed `requirements.txt` to `fastjson==3.1.0` and added a security note to `CHANGELOG.md`.\n- **CI:** Run ci-0001 passed on commit 597dec07c7.\n\nOne thing to check in review: main still pinned fastjson 2.8.0, so this PR is what actually moves the service onto 3.x. CI passed, but `src/money.py` doesn't use fastjson, so the tests I saw probably don't exercise it.\n\nI haven't merged the PR, because merging into main needs a code owner's approval.", "type": "text"}]}]}